<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="71968" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/PHIL/499/71968.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2025" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025.xml">2025</calendarYear><term id="120251" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring.xml">Spring 2025</term><subject id="PHIL" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/PHIL.xml">Philosophy</subject><course id="499" href="https://courses.illinois.edu/cisapp/explorer/schedule/2025/spring/PHIL/499.xml">Capstone Seminar</course></parents><sectionNumber>A  </sectionNumber><creditHours>3 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Hannah Arendt is one of the most original and important political thinkers of the 20th century. This course will focus on Hannah Arendt’s groundbreaking work on modernity, the human condition, and totalitarianism. Our key works will be The Human Condition and The Origins of Totalitarianism, but we will also read related selections from in The Jewish Writings, Responsibility and Judgment, The Life of the Mind, Lectures on Kant’s Political Philosophy, and Thinking without a Bannister.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Closed</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2025-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2025-05-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>03:20 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>313</roomNumber><buildingName>Gregory Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Varden" firstName="H">Varden, H</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>